	<!--
	  TCC usage descriptions for the per-user-data prompts that macOS
	  shows even for unsandboxed apps. Matisa reads project directories
	  configured in matisa.config.ts (typical locations include
	  ~/Desktop, ~/Documents, ~/Downloads, ~/Developer) to discover
	  what's running, so when the user's projects live under those
	  protected dirs the OS asks once on first launch. Strings show up
	  inside the prompt — give the user a meaningful reason rather than
	  the macOS default "Matisa.app would like to access…".

	  These only describe the prompt; the user still has to click
	  "Allow". For persistence across rebuilds we use a stable code
	  signing identity in build-app.sh — see that file's comments.
	-->
	<key>NSDesktopFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Matisa reads project directories listed in your matisa.config.ts. Allowing access lets it detect which services are running and tail their logs.</string>
	<key>NSDocumentsFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Matisa reads project directories listed in your matisa.config.ts. Allowing access lets it detect which services are running and tail their logs.</string>
	<key>NSDownloadsFolderUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Matisa reads project directories listed in your matisa.config.ts. Allowing access lets it detect which services are running and tail their logs.</string>
</dict>
